===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|merke}}, {{m|enm|mirke}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|mirce||dark, gloomy, evil}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*merkuz||dark}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*merg-||to flicker; darken; dark}}. Cognate with Danish {{m|da|mørk||dark}}, Norwegian {{m|no|mørk||dark}}, Swedish {{m|sv|mörk||dark}}, Icelandic {{m|is|myrkur||dark}}. Compare also Albanian {{m|sq|murg}}, Polish {{m|pl|mrok}} darkness, Lithuanian {{m|lt|márgas}} ‘multicolored’, Ancient Greek {{m|grc|ἀμορβός}} ‘dark’.

